---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Start here — The Sustainability Diet';
const description = 'If you read one page on this site, read this one. A two-minute version of the project: what the book argues, what the experiment asks, and where to go next.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical="https://sustainabilitydiet.com/start">
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16">

    <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">Start here · A two-minute read</p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-5xl md:text-6xl tracking-tight leading-[1.02] mb-8">
      If you read one page on this site, read this one.
    </h1>

    <div class="font-serif text-lg md:text-xl text-ink/85 leading-relaxed space-y-6">

      <p>
        <em>The Sustainability Diet</em> is a book in revision, forthcoming
        from Victory Belt. Its argument is simple and has two parts.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Part one.</strong> The dominant framework for judging food
        systems uses three axes — economic, social, environmental — and
        asks how each system behaves under roughly current conditions.
        That framework is incomplete. Most systems that score well on
        those three axes require something to keep going: diesel, federal
        subsidy, commodity grain, just-in-time supply chains, a stable
        climate. Remove any of those and the system stops. A fourth axis,
        <em>survivability</em>, asks what a food system requires to keep
        functioning, and what happens when those requirements fail. Most
        systems we call sustainable score poorly on survivability. Most
        systems that would score well are not in the conversation.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Part two.</strong> One family of food systems scores well
        on all four axes and is currently excluded from serious
        consideration: large-landscape rewilding using phylogenetically
        appropriate proxies for the megafauna that went extinct at the
        end of the Pleistocene. The book makes the case that this family
        is worth testing. It does not argue it is the answer. It argues
        it is a direction the evidence points and that the objections to
        testing it, examined carefully, are not as strong as they first
        appear.
      </p>

      <p>
        The book is also a tribute to Paul S. Martin, whose
        <em>Twilight of the Mammoths</em> (2005) is the intellectual
        headwater for this whole conversation and who died in 2010 without
        seeing his reframing enter the mainstream. The book is written so
        that a general reader, encountering him for the first time, can
        feel why.
      </p>

      <hr class="hairline my-10" />

      <p class="small-caps text-sm text-ochre-deep tracking-wide">The proposal</p>

      <p>
        The book ends with a specific, narrowly-defined experiment:
        one hundred dromedary camels on one hundred thousand acres of
        Great Plains or Southwestern rangeland, maintained for twenty-five
        years, with pre-registered failure criteria, measured outcomes, and
        an exit plan. Not a continental program. Not a rewriting of
        American land policy. A small, falsifiable test of whether a single
        proxy (<em>Camelus dromedarius</em>) can occupy part of the niche
        left by <em>Camelops hesternus</em>, the North American camel that
        went extinct ~13,000 years ago.
      </p>

      <p>
        The experiment is the book's ask. The rest of the site — the
        matrices, the timeline, the critics' arguments, the bibliography —
        exists to let a reader decide, without relying on the author's
        framing, whether the ask is reasonable.
      </p>

    </div>

